From: gt510 <gt510@localhost>
Subject: avcodec/v4l2m2m: report the rows below the picture as a bottom crop

0102 makes single-buffer DRM PRIME frames report the coded height (e.g.
1088 for 1080p, 384 for 360p) so Firefox finds the chroma plane at pitch *
frame->height. Firefox then showed those padding rows too: a green band
(all-zero YUV) along the bottom of the picture, 24 rows at 360p, slightly
squashed into the display size. Firefox sets apply_cropping = 0 and shows
frame->height - crop_bottom rows, so set crop_bottom to the padding.
Users with apply_cropping (the default) get the old height back from
av_frame_apply_cropping().
---
diff --git a/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c b/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
--- a/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
+++ b/libavcodec/v4l2_buffers.c
@@ -644,16 +644,20 @@
     /*
      * A DRM PRIME user (Firefox) finds the chroma plane of a single-buffer
      * NV12/YUV420 frame at pitch * frame->height, but the buffer holds the
-     * coded (aligned) height, e.g. 1088 rows for 1080p: report that height.
-     * A bottom crop would not help, av_frame_apply_cropping() folds it back
-     * into the height for hwaccel formats.
+     * coded (aligned) height, e.g. 1088 rows for 1080p or 384 for 360p:
+     * report that height, and the rows below the picture as a bottom crop.
+     * Firefox sets apply_cropping = 0, so the crop reaches it and it shows
+     * frame->height - crop_bottom rows; with apply_cropping (the default)
+     * av_frame_apply_cropping() folds the crop back into the height.
      */
     if (frame->format == AV_PIX_FMT_DRM_PRIME && avbuf->num_planes == 1) {
         int coded = V4L2_TYPE_IS_MULTIPLANAR(avbuf->context->type) ?
                     avbuf->context->format.fmt.pix_mp.height :
                     avbuf->context->format.fmt.pix.height;
-        if (coded > frame->height)
+        if (coded > frame->height) {
+            frame->crop_bottom = coded - frame->height;
             frame->height = coded;
+        }
     }
     frame->sample_aspect_ratio = avbuf->context->sample_aspect_ratio;
 
